            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> I'm trying to setup an autocmd to load a local session if it exists, and if not to open mini.starter. I have everything working except when the session does exist and is entered syntax highlighting doesn't work until i reload the buffer via `:e`. I'm new to autocmds, but I couldn't seem to find an answer to this problem.\r\n\r\nThis is indeed not an issue with 'mini.sessions', but an autocommand approach itself. The core issue comes from the fact that 'nvim-treesitter/nvim-treesitter' enables highlighting in the autocommand for `FileType` event. When `MiniSessions.read()` is executed inside a `VimEnter` autocommand, it means that all other events are not triggered because \"By default, autocommands do not nest.\" (see `:h autocmd-nested`). This includes a `FileType` events, which means that 'nvim-treesitter' does not start its highlighting.\r\n\r\nThe solution is very simple: add `nested = true` above `callback = function()` line for your `VImEnter` autocommand. And there is not need for `post` hook anymore.\r\n\r\nThe way this could have been deduced (how I ended up doing it) is by first checking that `autoread` in 'mini.sessions' works as expected here. Then take a look at [its implementation](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/608e15779f3d672d30c8ee01ad8ff9cac9f7d723/lua/mini/sessions.lua#L453) and notice the `nested = true` flag.",
